From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Derek Ross <derekross@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 08:55:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] soc: google: cpif: port the packet processor

Build Google's packet processor (link_rx_pktproc.c, link_tx_pktproc.c)
behind CONFIG_CP_PKTPROC and CONFIG_CP_PKTPROC_UL, both off by default,
with CONFIG_LINK_DEVICE_PCIE_IOCC on as stock has it: the gs201 modem
link driver turns IO coherency on at every link up, before the modem can
DMA. Stock gs201 runs four downlink and two uplink queues in the PKTPROC
and PKTPROC_UL regions.

Port it to this kernel:

- The regions are reserved no-map here, outside the linear map stock's
  phys_to_virt() relies on. Map the info, descriptor and buffer parts
  with memremap(), write-back where the device tree calls them cached,
  write-combine otherwise, and unmap them with memunmap() on failure
  (cp_shmem_get_nc_region() is memremap() too since the port).
- Check the device tree's offsets and sizes against the regions, the
  queue and packet sizes, and that the descriptors fit, before mapping or
  dividing by them; refuse the on-SoC modems' dedicated BAAW window.
- Allocate the per-queue dummy NAPI devices (alloc_netdev_dummy()), and
  free them on failure.
- Check that the whole packet a descriptor points at, not just its
  start, lies in the queue's buffer.
- Return errors where Google panics on a device tree that does not
  match the build.

Stock decides at build time that the modem uses the packet processor:
with CONFIG_CP_PKTPROC_UL every packet goes out without a link header,
and the AP advertises the uplink processor at boot. Make that follow the
device tree instead (pktproc_dl_support, pktproc_ul_support), so that a
kernel with the options also runs the modem on its legacy buffers, as
before, when the modem node does not ask for the packet processor: the
capability bit, the uplink path and the missing link header depend on
the uplink processor having been set up; the network devices get at
least one receive queue, and resume does not look at queues that do not
exist. vnet_xmit() no longer reads uninitialised configuration bytes on
the SIT path.

The queues' own MSI vectors (pktproc_dl_use_exclusive_irq, as stock) are
not available yet: the next commit adds them.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Derek Ross <derekross@gmail.com>
